[Music]  stereotypes and word choices often go  hand-in-hand for example a recent study  of massive text datasets found that in  the early 1900's Asians in the US were  characterized with words like barbaric  or monstrous  but that today words like passive and  sensitive are more common we see this  play out in the workplace as well job  ads for professional roles are often  peppered with stereotypically masculine  words as our performance reviews and  letters of recommendation these patterns  have consequences we know that when  people are described in ways that  reflect stereotypes stereotypes are not  only reinforced but they can also create  disadvantages  as an educator in a business school I  wanted to know if the case studies we  use in our classes also reflect  stereotypes so a team of undergraduate  research assistants and I read the 250  unique case studies taught in the GSB  MBA core curriculum the goal was to  analyze the text of each case study by  scrutinizing the descriptions of the  protagonists their situations and their  cultural context to look for any  potentially stereotypical language or  language patterns  we discovered a number of ways in which  stereotypes creep into the language  that's used in these case studies first  some case studies make sweeping  statements about entire countries for  example one case study includes comments  about the alleged rigid hierarchy of  Japan based on class age and education  another case references how physically  attractive Brazilians are here rather  than making sweeping statements examples  and data could instead be provided to  support the point being made in the case  study similarly some case studies fail  to provide sufficient context around  statements and wind up just reinforcing  these stereotypes for example on a case  study on an erectile dysfunction drug  there's discussion about how Eady leads  to a reduction in feeling masculine and  there's a reference to a report in the  case study that says that over 90% of  men and women reported that confidence  in a man's sexual ability is critical to  having a good loving relationship here  the case study could have provided a  little bit more context for example by  acknowledging that the study that they  are cited included only heterosexual  couples  and while the comments about a reduction  in feeling masculine may be directly  from the study that they cited this type  of statement can reinforce the  stereotype that a man's sexual ability  is and should be important to his sense  of masculinity third case study language  can promote and reinforce gender racial  and ethnic stereotypes for example a  case on a woman CEO opens up with her  reading to her three-year-old son and  kissing him goodnight before joining her  husband in the living room or another  case quotes a CEO as saying that the  best lunches in Japan are prepared by  wives at home most of these examples  reinforce the stereotype that women  should be wives and mothers but not  leaders  [Music]  first we should train case writers about  how easy it is for stereotypes to creep  into case studies so that they can be  attuned to the language of leadership  and strive to make sure that the case  studies they write do not inadvertently  reinforce stereotypes we should also  insist that whenever possible they  include relevant context and data when  making sweeping claims here at the  Stanford GSB we're already doing this  we're working on training all of our  case writers on the best practices  second for those of us using case  studies in the classroom we should  conduct an audit of language used in all  cases that we assign this is something  we're also doing here at the GSB we are  currently writing cover memos for any of  the cases that we found with potentially  problematic language and we're making  these memos available to faculty well we  obviously can't do this for all of our  cases we believe that faculty once  they're made aware of these issues will  begin to audit all of the cases that  they use  third as educators we can create a  simple rubric to help students see how  they might identify stereotypes and case  studies we can teach them to see if any  of the patterns we identified can be  found in any particular case they're  reading for example we can ask them to  underline country descriptions and  descriptions of protagonists and then  evaluate these for any sort of bias  finally as educators it's important for  us to make this a teaching moment once  we have raised issues of bias and the  case studies we can facilitate a  discussion of how language impacts other  managerial situations that all of our  students will experience in their  futures  for example bias can creep into writing  job descriptions letters of reference  hiring briefs or performance evaluations  we should teach them to scrutinize the  language that they use as learners  you  [Music]

